Shell Redirection & the sed Stream Editor
Combined Date and User Redirection
To display the current date on the screen while simultaneously redirecting the list of logged-in users to a file, use the semicolon ; or logical AND && operator in a single line:
Command:
date; who > logged_users.txt
Explanation:
date: Prints the current system date and time to the standard output (your screen).
;: Separates the two commands so they run sequentially.
who: Lists the users currently logged in.
> logged_users.txt: Redirects the output of the who command into a file named logged_users.txt.
Note: Ho sakta hai ki 'who' command har environment mein available ya reliable na ho, kyunki yeh system ki configuration par depend karta hai. Iske alternative mein aap 'w' ya 'whoami' command ka use kar sakte hain.
Basic sed Commands
A Conceptual, Classroom-Friendly Guide to the Stream Editor
1. What is sed?
sed stands for Stream Editor.
It is a Linux command-line utility used to read and transform text. It can perform operations such as:
Printing selected lines
Deleting lines
Searching for text
Replacing text
Modifying text using patterns
Performing automatic text transformations
Unlike a text editor such as nano, sed normally does not open the file for interactive editing. Instead, it reads the input, applies the specified operation, and produces the resulting output.
Printing Text Using sed
2. How to Display the Contents of a File Using sed?
Command
sed -n 'p' Names.txt
Explanation
The command is made up of three parts:
sed     -n     'p'     Names.txt
|        |       |          |
|        |       |          +-- File to process
|        |       +------------- Print command
|        +--------------------- Suppress automatic printing
+------------------------------ Stream editor
sed
Starts the Stream Editor.
-n
Normally, sed automatically prints every line after processing it.
The -n option means:
Do not print lines automatically.
This allows us to control exactly which lines are printed.
p
p means print.
Because -n has disabled automatic printing, the p command tells sed which lines we specifically want to display.
Names.txt
This is the input file.
Important
-n -> suppress automatic output
p  -> explicitly print
Therefore:
sed -n 'p' Names.txt
prints every line exactly once.
3. How to Display a Specific Line?
Command
sed -n '2p' Names.txt
This prints only line number 2.
Suppose Names.txt contains:
John Doe
Alice Smith
Rahul Sharma
Aniket Singh
Output:
Alice Smith
Explanation
2p
| |
| +-- print
+---- line number 2
So:
2p -> print line 2
The -n option is important because it prevents the other lines from being printed automatically.
4. How to Display a Range of Lines?
Command
sed -n '2,4p' Names.txt
This prints lines 2 through 4.
Output:
Alice Smith
Rahul Sharma
Aniket Singh
Explanation
2,4p
| | |
| | +-- print
| +---- ending line
+------ starting line
Therefore:
2,4p -> print lines 2 to 4
Deleting Lines Using sed
5. How to Delete a Specific Line?
Command
sed '2d' Names.txt
This removes line 2 from the output produced by sed.
Explanation
2d
| |
| +-- delete
+---- line 2
d means delete.
If the file contains:
John Doe
Alice Smith
Rahul Sharma
Aniket Singh
the output becomes:
John Doe
Rahul Sharma
Aniket Singh
Important
This command does not permanently remove line 2 from Names.txt.
It only removes the line from the output.
To modify the original file, -i must be used:
sed -i '2d' Names.txt
6. How to Delete the First and Last Line?
Command
sed '1d' Names.txt
sed '$d' Names.txt
This means:
1 -> line number 1
d -> delete
Therefore:
1d -> delete line 1
The remaining lines are displayed.
Substituting Text
7. How to Replace a Word Using sed?
Command
sed 's/John/Amit/' Names.txt
Suppose the file contains:
John Doe
Alice Smith
Rahul Sharma
Output:
Amit Doe
Alice Smith
Rahul Sharma
Explanation
The general syntax is:
s/old_text/new_text/
For our command:
s/John/Amit/
| |    |
| |    +-- replacement
| +------- text to search
+--------- substitute
Therefore:
s -> substitute
John -> search for this text for the first occurence
Amit -> replace it with this text
8. What Happens When the Same Word Appears Multiple Times?
Suppose:
John Doe
John Smith
Rahul John
Run:
sed 's/John/Amit/' Names.txt
Output:
Amit Doe
Amit Smith
Rahul John
By default, sed replaces only the first occurrence on each line.
9. How to Replace All Occurrences in a Line?
Use the g flag.
Command
sed 's/John/Amit/g' Names.txt
Output:
Amit Doe
Amit Smith
Rahul Amit
Explanation
g -> global
It tells sed:
Continue replacing matches instead of stopping after the first match on each line.
Compare:
sed 's/John/Amit/' Names.txt
-> first occurrence on each line.
sed 's/John/Amit/g' Names.txt
-> all occurrences on each line.
Editing the Original File
10. How to Permanently Apply the Changes?
Normally:
sed 's/John/Amit/g' Names.txt
only displays the modified result. The original file remains unchanged.
To modify the actual file, use:
sed -i 's/John/Amit/g' Names.txt
Explanation
-i -> in-place editing
It tells sed to write the changes back into the original file.
Difference
Command
Effect
sed 's/John/Amit/g' Names.txt
Changes only the output.
sed -i 's/John/Amit/g' Names.txt
Changes the actual file.


11. How to Create a Backup Before Editing?
Command
sed -i.bak 's/John/Amit/g' Names.txt
This performs the replacement and creates:
Names.txt
Names.txt.bak
The .bak file contains the original version.
This is useful when testing commands because you have a backup if something goes wrong.
Using Special Characters
12. How to Match Text at the Beginning/End of a Line?
Use:
^
Command
sed 's/^John/Amit/' Names.txt
Suppose:
John Doe
My friend John
John Smith
Output:
Amit Doe
My friend John
Amit Smith
Explanation
^ -> beginning of the line
Therefore:
^John
means:
Match John only when it occurs at the beginning of a line.
“My friend John” is not changed because John is not at the beginning.
13. How to Match Text at the End of a Line?
Use:
$
Command
sed 's/John$/Amit/' Names.txt
Suppose:
John Doe
My friend John
John Smith
Output:
John Doe
My friend Amit
John Smith
Explanation
$ -> end of the line
Therefore:
John$
means:
Match John only when it occurs at the end of a line.
Working with Spaces
14. How to Replace All Spaces with _?
Command
sed 's/ /_/g' Names.txt
Suppose:
John Doe
Alice Smith
Rahul Sharma
Output:
John_Doe
Alice_Smith
Rahul_Sharma
Explanation
The command:
s/ /_/g
contains:
s -> substitute
' ' -> a space
_ -> replacement character
g -> all occurrences
So every space on every line is replaced with _.
15. How to Replace Only the First Space?
Command
sed 's/ /_/' Names.txt
If the line is:
John Michael Doe
the output is:
John_Michael Doe
Only the first space is replaced.
Difference
Command
Effect
sed 's/ /_/' Names.txt
Replaces the first matching space on each line.
sed 's/ /_/g' Names.txt
Replaces every matching space on each line.


Regular Expressions
16. What Does . Mean in sed?
The dot . represents any single character.
Suppose:
cat
bat
hat
dog
Run:
sed 's/.at/XXX/' file.txt
Output:
XXX
XXX
XXX
dog
Why?
The pattern:
.at
means:
. -> any one character
a -> the letter a
t -> the letter t
Therefore it matches cat, bat, and hat — but not dog.
17. What Does [^ ] Mean?
The expression [^ ] means:
Match any character that is not a space.
The ^ inside the square brackets has a different meaning from the ^ used at the beginning of a pattern.
For example, [^ ] means not a space.
When we add *:
[^ ]*
it means:
Match zero or more characters that are not spaces.
This is commonly used to match a word.
18. How to Match the First Word of Each Line?
Command
sed 's/[^ ]*/WORD/' Names.txt
Suppose:
John Doe
Alice Smith
Rahul Sharma
Output:
WORD Doe
WORD Smith
WORD Sharma
Explanation
[^ ] -> any character except a space.
* -> repeat the previous pattern zero or more times.
Therefore [^ ]* matches characters until a space is encountered.
For John Doe it matches John.
Capturing Groups
19. What is a Group in sed?
A group allows us to capture a portion of the text so that we can use that matched text again in the replacement.
In basic sed regular expressions, groups are written as:
\(...\)
For example:
sed 's/\([^ ]*\)/{\1}/' Names.txt
If the file contains:
John Doe
Alice Smith
output:
[John] Doe
[Alice] Smith
Explanation
\([^ ]*\)
captures the first word. This becomes Group 1.
We can refer to Group 1 using:
\1
So \1 means:
Insert whatever text was captured by Group 1.
20. How to Capture Two Words?
Command
sed 's/\([^ ]*\) \([^ ]*\)/{\1}{\2}/' Names.txt
Suppose:
John Doe
Alice Smith
Output:
John-Doe
Alice-Smith
Understanding the Pattern
\([^ ]*\) \([^ ]*\)
contains two groups.
First group \([^ ]*\) -> captures the first word.
Second group \([^ ]*\) -> captures the second word.
The replacement is:
\1-\2
where:
\1 -> first captured group
\2 -> second captured group
Therefore John Doe becomes John-Doe.
Final Experiment: Swapping Two Words
21. How to Swap the First Two Words of Every Line?
Command
sed 's/^\([^ ]*\) \([^ ]*\)/\2 \1/' Names.txt
Suppose Names.txt contains:
John Doe
Alice Smith
Rahul Sharma
Aniket Singh
Output:
Doe John
Smith Alice
Sharma Rahul
Singh Aniket
Detailed Explanation
The search pattern is:
^\([^ ]*\) \([^ ]*\)
Break it into parts:
Pattern piece
Meaning
^
Start at the beginning of the line.
\([^ ]*\)
Capture the first word as Group 1.
[space]
Match the space between the first and second words.
\([^ ]*\)
Capture the second word as Group 2.


So for John Doe, sed identifies:
Group 1 -> John
Group 2 -> Doe
The replacement is:
\2 \1
Therefore:
Group 2 + space + Group 1
becomes:
Doe John
The same process is performed for every line.
Complete Working Example
Create the file:
nano Names.txt
Enter:
John Doe
Alice Smith
Rahul Sharma
Aniket Singh
Save the file. Run:
sed 's/^\([^ ]*\) \([^ ]*\)/\2 \1/' Names.txt
Output:
Doe John
Smith Alice
Sharma Rahul
Singh Aniket
To Modify the Original File
Use:
sed -i 's/^\([^ ]*\) \([^ ]*\)/\2 \1/' Names.txt
Now Names.txt itself will contain:
Doe John
Smith Alice
Sharma Rahul
Singh Aniket
