Key Idea to Remember
The final command:
sed 's/^\([^ ]*\) \([^ ]*\)/\2 \1/' Names.txt
can be understood as:
Find:
    beginning -> first word -> space -> second word
Store:
    first word  -> Group 1
    second word -> Group 2
Replace with:
    Group 2 -> space -> Group 1
Therefore John Doe becomes Doe John.
The most important idea is that sed first identifies the text using a pattern and then constructs the replacement using the captured groups.
Swapping Words with Sed
sed (stream editor) is a powerful utility for text manipulation that allows you to reorder or modify data streams efficiently. A common use case is automatically switching the positions of the first two words on every line of a file.
The Command:
sed -i 's/^\([^ ]*\) \([^ ]*\)/\2 \1/' Names.txt
Example: Running this on a file containing "John Doe" will instantly change the line to "Doe John".
What happens:
The command processes names.txt line by line, identifying the first word (group 1) and the second word (group 2) via the regular expression ^([^ ]*) ([^ ]*).
The replacement pattern \2 \1 swaps their positions, and the -i flag saves these modifications directly to the file.
Pro Tip: Use sed -i.bak to create a backup of the original file before applying changes.
Breakdown of the Sed Syntax
The command sed -i 's/\([^ ]*\) \([^ ]*\)/\2 \1/' filename.txt is composed of several powerful elements:
sed: Invokes the stream editor utility to process text data.
-i: The "in-place" flag, which saves all modifications directly to the target file.
's/ ... / ... /': Denotes the substitution operation
^: An anchor specifying the beginning of a text line.
\([^ ]*\): Group 1, which identifies the initial word by matching characters that are not spaces.
(space): Explicitly matches the literal space character between distinct words.
\([^ ]*\): Group 2, which isolates the subsequent word in the sequence.
\2 \1: The replacement order. It puts the second word (\2) first, then a space, followed by the first word (\1).
